GENERAL BACKGROUND (reference text)

Paper is produced by pressing together the cellulose fibers of wood, rags, or
grasses and drying them into flexible sheets. The pulp is first suspended in
water, then spread across a fine mesh screen so the liquid drains away while
the fibers settle into a thin, even mat. Rollers press out the remaining
moisture and heated cylinders finish the drying, after which the continuous
sheet is wound onto large reels for cutting.

The properties of the finished sheet depend on the length of the fibers, the
degree of beating the pulp received, and any coatings applied at the end of
the process. Longer fibers yield stronger paper, while heavier beating
produces a denser, smoother surface. Additives such as clay or chalk improve
opacity and brightness, and a thin surface sizing keeps ink from spreading
along the fibers.

Recycled stock follows the same path once the used paper has been shredded,
mixed with water, and cleaned of inks and adhesives. Each cycle shortens the
fibers slightly, so recycled pulp is commonly blended with a portion of new
fiber to keep the sheet strong enough for printing and folding. Modern mills
monitor the moving sheet continuously, adjusting pulp flow, roller pressure,
and drying temperature so that thickness and moisture stay uniform from one
end of the reel to the other.

